l to the nearest tenth. $$\\begin{array}{c|c}\nx & y \\\\ \\hline\n2 & -5 \\\\\np & -14 \\\\\np+2 & -17 \\\\\n\\end{array}$$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"186430645c0f68b596f8c7c401a083480c4f1c4395ae45850e207a21991af72d"},"seconds":17.033406019210815,"seed":85376775404665462,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the value of \\(p + q\\), we need to find the equation of the line that passes through the given points. We start by identifying two points from the table to find the slope of the line. The points \\((2, -5)\\) and \\((p, -14)\\) can be used to find the slope \\(m\\).\n\nThe slope \\(m\\) is given by:\n\\[\nm = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} = \\frac{-14 - (-5)}{p - 2} = \\frac{-14 + 5}{p - 2} = \\frac{-9}{p - 2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the point-slope form of the line equation \\(y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)\\) with the point \\((2, -5)\\):\n\\[\ny + 5 = \\frac{-9}{p - 2}(x - 2)\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{-9}{p - 2}(x - 2) - 5\n\\]\n\nWe can also use the points \\((p, -14)\\) and \\((p+2, -17)\\) to find the slope \\(m\\) again:\n\\[\nm = \\frac{-17 - (-14)}{(p+2) - p} = \\frac{-17 + 14}{2} = \\frac{-3}{2}\n\\]\nSo the slope \\(m\\) is \\(-\\frac{3}{2}\\). Now we can use the point \\((2, -5)\\) to find the equation of the line:\n\\[\ny + 5 = -\\frac{3}{2}(x - 2)\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\ny + 5 = -\\frac{3}{2}x + 3\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{3}{2}x - 2\n\\]\n\nNow we can find the value of \\(p\\) by substituting \\(x = p\\) into the line equation:\n\\[\n-14 = -\\frac{3}{2}p - 2\n\\]\nSolving for \\(p\\), we get:\n\\[\n-14 + 2 = -\\frac{3}{2}p\n\\]\n\\[\n-12 = -\\frac{3}{2}p\n\\]\n\\[\np = \\frac{-12 \\cdot 2}{-3} = 8\n\\]\n\nNow we find the value of \\(q\\) by substituting \\(x = 13\\) into the line equation:\n\\[\nq = -\\frac{3}{2}(13) - 2 = -\\frac{39}{2} - 2 = -19.5 - 2 = -21.5\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find \\(p + q\\):\n\\[\np + q = 8 + (-21.5) = -13.5\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(p + q\\) is \\(\\boxed{-13.5}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-13.5}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:33"}
